AB  - Recent developments in reasoning-oriented large language models (LLMs) have opened the range of uses of Natural Language Processing (NLP) both in academia and industry. Among these models, DeepSeek has stood out as an interesting open-source model that can compute complex reasoning tasks with lower computational overhead. Despite its growing popularity, the large-scale implementation of DeepSeek is still bound by multiple technical, organizational and regulatory barriers. This study offers an overview of the key obstacles to DeepSeek adoption. Based on a thorough review of the most recent journal literature (indexed) and insights from domain experts in AI and machine learning, sixteen barriers are identified. Further, these barriers are divided into four categories according to these four problems: technical, safeguard, normative and interpretability problems. To prioritize these barriers, the analytic hierarchy process is used with a web-based survey filled by the students of university, which can effectively carry out the uncertainties of the expert judgement. The results show that the major factors that hinder adoption include the 'Accountability', 'Usability' and 'Customization'. The proposed framework gives a comprehensive understanding of the relative importance of the barriers of adoption and some helpful suggestions for the researchers, developers and decision-makers who are looking to enable the secure, scalable and responsible deployment of systems using DeepSeek.
ER  - 

