AB  - Spam comments have been flooding the Internet on social media, blogs, and e-commerce sites, threatening normal user experience, data integrity and system security. Rules based and keyword matching-based methods are no longer effective, as spammers are resorting to more complex evasion techniques to combat filtering systems. Artificial Intelligence (AI), and Machine Learning (ML) and Deep Learning (DL) methods, have arisen as the most successful option to identify spam with high accuracy and growing versatility. This survey paper has been aimed to make an extensive assessment on the available AI-based techniques of spam comment detection. For each model, we compare approaches ranging from classical ML methods like the Naïve Bayes and Support Vector Machines, to DL such as the LSTM and CNN, as well as the recent addition of the NLP models which include BERT and Word2Vec. We also analyze hybrid solutions, ensemble techniques, and the increasing use of large language models and transformer-based architectures. The paper details the main novelties, like real-time detection pipelines, graph- based filtering and the use of explain- able AI (XAI) to improve trust and interpretability. We also describe the present limitations, such as dataset bias and multilingual challenge, and adversarial spam, as well as sketch the future research areas including federated learning and cross- domain generalization. In this survey, we hope to help researchers and industrial practitioners in constructing more effective and smarter spam filtering systems.
ER  - 

