AB  - <p>Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed natural language processing (NLP), but their unclear reasoning processes and tendency to hallucinate present challenges for trustworthy information processing. In legal applications, while LLMs show promise for text analysis and generation, they struggle with reliable citation attribution and precedent verification, raising concerns about their use in high&#x2010;stakes judicial systems and the legal domain.</p> <p>Current methods to improve LLM trustworthiness in legal domains often lack comprehensive legal knowledge integration, either through end&#x2010;to&#x2010;end training or instruction tuning. Additionally, there are insufficient mechanisms to verify the accuracy of LLM&#x2010;generated outputs, such as legal citations and precedents.</p> <p>In this chapter, we propose addressing these challenges with the TRISM (Trustworthy, Reliable, Interpretable, Safe Models) framework, which combines Neurosymbolic AI principles with LLMs. We outline the current limitations of using Generative AI as a legal assistant and identify potential opportunities for further research along with a brief survey of recent approaches, specifically in the legal domain, that utilize neural and Neurosymbolic methods. Finally, we formalize the process of creating symbolic knowledge from legal textual documents and emphasize the role of Retrieval&#x2010;Augmented Generation (RAG) in advancing legal AI applications to follow TRISM framework.</p>
ER  - 

