AB  - This work presents a survey of two promising emergent research topics in child speech processing and recognition: Child Speaker Verification (CSV)and Automatic Pronunciation Assessment (APA). We begin by explaining what is so tricky about children’s speech for machines to process. Their voices change as they grow, and there is a lot of variation from one child to another. We then describe how CSV systems have evolved over the years from initial models that preferred not to see basic acoustic mismatch new schemes to newer approaches based on better data augmentation, improved feature modeling,and designs that are less age based. We then turn to APA, beginning with older ASR based scoring methods and progressing to more modern deep learning systems, self supervised models, Transformer based approaches,and newer tools that utilize computational models that are large language models to generate feedback that is more comprehendible by learners and clinicians. Finally, we look at how these two areas intersect in education and clinical practice. APA assists with learning to read, respect and forcing pronunciation every DIR child deserves the best opportunity and APA helps kids learn to read text. We end with ongoing challenges such as the non availability of long term datasets, poor modeling system of prosody, and need of more inclusive systems.
